summary_en.technical_approach:
ClawGuard is a runtime security framework enforcing a user-confirmed rule set at every tool-call boundary:
1) One-time pre-session rule induction: before the first tool call, automatically derive task-specific access constraints from the user’s objective and have the user confirm them, producing ℛ_task that is uncontaminated by adversarial tool outputs.
2) Per-tool-call enforcement with ℛ = ℛ_base ∪ ℛ_task via four components:
- Content Sanitizer: redact sensitive spans from outgoing tool arguments and incoming tool outputs before they enter the conversation history;
- Rule Evaluator: check each proposed tool call against rules covering tool invocations, local file paths, and outbound network destinations;
- Skill Inspector: automated risk assessment + mandatory user confirmation before first skill execution;
- Approval Mechanism + Audit Log: route ambiguous calls to the user for explicit authorization and record all events for auditing.
3) Design goals: model-agnostic, protocol-agnostic, and compatible with runtime tool selection while enforcing deterministic guardrails at the boundary.

summary_en.reading_guide:
must_read (deep read):
- Key sections: Introduction (three injection channels + limitations of prior defenses), framework design (rule induction from user goals, rule representation/coverage, component I/O and insertion points), and security analysis (why boundary enforcement blocks tool-output-borne instructions).
- Key figures/materials: architecture diagram showing the tool-call interception point and data flow; concrete rule examples for ℛ_base/ℛ_task; main results tables on the three benchmarks; ablations removing Sanitizer/Approval/Skill Inspector; case studies tracing how typical injections are intercepted.
- Innovation checklist: (i) pre-session, uncontaminated task rule induction + user confirmation, (ii) deterministic boundary enforcement rather than probabilistic model resistance, (iii) unified coverage of realistic MCP/skill ecosystem attack surfaces.

skim (quick read):
- Read Introduction to align threat channels;
- Read the system overview + the four components list;
- Jump to main results and a representative case study;
- Skim deployment/compatibility and overhead discussion.
